## [ISSUE] A missing English bundle stops the verifier scan with an unchecked RuntimeException instead of being reported as a validation error

**Severity:** LOW - a robustness and consistency gap. No current bundle triggers it; a future translation-only bundle would abort the scan at the first such file.

### The Problem
`VerifyMessageProperties.java:74-79` (line 70 has the same pattern for the translated file):
```java
String englishFile = file.getAbsolutePath().replaceAll("resources-community", "resources")
        .replaceAll("_[a-zA-Z-_]*\\.properties", "_en.properties");
try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(englishFile)) { ... }
catch (IOException e) { throw new RuntimeException("unable to read file " + englishFile, e); }
```
`verify()` only catches `IOException`, and `ThemeVerifierMojo.execute()` has no try/catch. So if the English counterpart is missing, the whole scan stops at that file, and no other bundle's validation messages are collected. Every other problem this class detects is added to `messages` and reported together.

### Theory / Proof / what breaks
All current `messages_*.properties` under `themes/src/main/resources*` and `js/apps/*/maven-resources*` map to an existing `_en` file, so nothing triggers this today. The trigger would be a locale-only bundle, or a new resource directory layout. The build would then fail with "unable to read file ..." and the path, which is clear enough. However, other translation problems in the same run would be hidden until the next build.

### Recommendation
Record it as a validation message so the scan continues. Switching to `MojoExecutionException` would behave almost the same as today.
```java
File englishFile = new File(file.getAbsolutePath().replaceAll("resources-community", "resources")
        .replaceAll("_[a-zA-Z-_]*\\.properties", "_en.properties"));
if (!englishFile.exists()) {
    messages.add("No English source bundle " + englishFile + " found for " + file);
    return;
}
```
